  66: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
  67:     """
  68:     Slide each solid rectangular body by the length and direction of its one-cell-thick tail, then redraw the scene with sliders on top.
  69: 
  70:     Input structure
  71:     - The background is black.
  72:     - Each foreground color represents one object: either a filled rectangle, possibly partly hidden by another color, or a filled rectangle with a straight one-cell-thick tail of length at least two.
  73:     - Slider tails extend in one cardinal direction from one side of their rectangle; shifted slider rectangles remain in bounds and do not overlap one another.
  74: 
  75:     Rule
  76:     - Restore every non-slider object as the filled rectangle of its input bounding box.
  77:     - For every slider, discard the tail and translate only the rectangular body by the tail length in the tail direction.
  78:     - Draw the translated slider rectangles over the restored static rectangles.
  79: 
  80:     Key insight
  81:     - A thin tail is a displacement vector for its rectangle, while wider missing areas inside another color's bounding box are occlusion that should be restored.
  82:     """
